   "tutor": "Here is the rule: use an before a word that starts with a vowel sound (an egg, an hour), and a before others.\nRight: \"An owl sleeps all day.\" Wrong: \"A owl sleeps all day.\" The wrong one uses a, but owl starts with a vowel sound.\nWorked example: \"The zoo has a camel.\" First, I say the noun: camel. Next, I check its first sound: a c sound, not a vowel sound. So the article is a.\nNow try it the same way. Write a or an: She has ___ orange in her bag. (answer with only the missing word)",
